Get density statistics per residue range (Python interface).

PRIMARY FUNCTION FOR FINDING POORLY-FITTED RESIDUES

This is the main function to use when asked \"Which side chain is worst fitting to density?\" It analyzes correlation statistics for all residues in a chain at once. For each run of n_residue_per_residue_range consecutive residues, the correlation between the observed map and a map calculated from the model is computed (excluding grid points that are also covered by residues outside the run) and reported against the middle residue of the run. The grid points used are those within 2.8 Å of the atoms of the run. Runs that include waters, nucleotides or HETATM residues are skipped.

  imol  Model molecule number   chain_id  Chain identifier (e.g., \"A\", \"B\")   imol_map  Map molecule number   n_residue_per_residue_range  Number of residues per analysis window:  Use 1 for per-residue statistics (most common) Use 3 for smoothed statistics over 3-residue windows   exclude_mainchain_NOC_flag  Whether to split off the main chain:  0: use all grid points; the first list has the all-atom statistics and the second list has empty statistics (n_points = 0, correlation nan) 1: the split is made by atom, not by grid point: the grid points around atoms that lie within 1.8 Å of a main-chain N, C, O or H atom (i.e. N, C, O, H and also CA, and the CD of proline) go into the first list (which is then main-chain statistics) and the grid points around all other atoms (CB onwards) go into the second (side-chain) list a list of two lists [first_stats, sidechain_stats]. Each is a list of items [residue_spec, [n_points, correlation]] where residue_spec is [chain_id, res_no, ins_code]; the items are sorted by residue spec. Both lists are empty if imol or imol_map is not valid. n_points is the number of atom/grid-point contributions: a grid point that is within 2.8 Å of several atoms is counted once for each of them (and, with exclude_mainchain_NOC_flag = 1, it may be counted in both lists).

If the residue does not have a side-chain then the number of grid points may be 0 and the correlation is then nan.

This function analyzes the ENTIRE chain at once, making it very efficient Use exclude_mainchain_NOC_flag = 1 and the second list to identify problem side chains specifically Example usage - Find worst-fitting side chain: # Get correlation statistics for all residues in chain A # (args: imol, chain_id, imol_map, n_residue_per_residue_range, exclude_mainchain_NOC_flag) mainchain_stats, sidechain_stats = coot.map_to_model_correlation_stats_per_residue_range_py(1, \"A\" , 2, 1, 1) # Find worst-fitting side chain (ignoring residues with no side-chain grid points) scored = [item for item in sidechain_stats if item[1][0] > 0] worst_residue = min(scored, key= lambda item: item[1][1]) chain_id, resno, ins_code = worst_residue[0] correlation = worst_residue[1][1] print(f \"Worst side chain: {chain_id} {resno}, correlation = {correlation:.3f}\" ) # Center on worst residue coot.set_go_to_atom_chain_residue_atom_name(chain_id, resno, 'CA' )

Example usage - Compare main-chain vs side-chain fit: mainchain, sidechain = coot.map_to_model_correlation_stats_per_residue_range_py(1, \"A\" , 2, 1, 1) side_corr = {tuple(spec): stats[1] for spec, stats in sidechain} for spec, stats in mainchain: mc_corr = stats[1] sc_corr = side_corr.get(tuple(spec)) if sc_corr is not None and mc_corr > 0.7 and sc_corr < 0.5: print(f \"Residue {spec}: Good backbone, poor sidechain\" )

Parameters
----------
imol : int
    Model molecule number
chain_id : str
    Chain identifier (e.g., \"A\", \"B\")
imol_map : int
    Map molecule number
n_residue_per_residue_range : int
    Number of residues per analysis window:  Use 1 for per-residue statistics (most common) Use 3 for smoothed statistics over 3-residue windows
exclude_mainchain_NOC_flag : int
    Whether to split off the main chain:  0: use all grid points; the first list has the all-atom statistics and the second list has empty statistics (n_points = 0, correlation nan) 1: the split is made by atom, not by grid point: the grid points around atoms that lie within 1.8 Å of a main-chain N, C, O or H atom (i.e. N, C, O, H and also CA, and the CD of proline) go into the first list (which is then main-chain statistics) and the grid points around all other atoms (CB onwards) go into the second (side-chain) list
";

